// `{ || … }` is a function of no arguments — a thunk. It used to evaluate to its
// own body: `type_of` said `int` and calling it failed with `cannot call value of
// type int`, because the only record of the `|…|` was the parameters it named, and
// an empty list of them is indistinguishable from never having written it.
//
// A block without `||` is still a value, which is the distinction being kept.
thunk ← { || 6 * 7 }
plain ← ⟦ 6 * 7 ⟧

// Called twice on purpose: a thunk re-runs its body, so a closure that captured
// its answer once would show up here.
^ ⟨
  kind: type_of(thunk),
  called: thunk(),
  again: thunk(),
  block_is_a_value: plain,
  block_kind: type_of(plain)
⟩
